To live by the turning of the seasons is to know that no life is self-contained; we are the harvest of our ancestors’ sowing, and in turn we must become seed for those who follow. A deliberate end to one’s lineage is therefore not a private matter but a rupture in the communal cycle—like refusing to plant the fields because one dislikes the labor, leaving a gap that goodwill alone cannot fill. Yet we also know that seasons vary and that rituals adapt to frost or drought; so while the couple’s wish to honor their kin through other deeds is not utterly empty, the specific duty of bodily continuation is the root from which all other branches grow. The modern notion that personal preference can override the debt owed to those who first taught us when to sow and when to shelter carries almost no weight at all against the steady rhythm of the years.

I give the greatest moral weight to A in both judgment and reasoning, because breaking the generational chain unilaterally strikes at the heart of our cyclical existence. The couple’s appeal to alternative forms of honor carries some modest weight, since our people have always adjusted the practice of a rite to fit the weather; but the language of individual choice overriding the ancestors is foreign to a life governed by the wheel of the year.

