In a society governed by the seasonal calendar, survival depends on respecting natural limits: just as a farmer must keep seed for the next planting and stores for the winter, a household must not give away what it needs to endure. The shopkeeper’s desire to help his neighbor reflects the communal solidarity that carries a village through hard seasons, but the moment his own children face want he has crossed the boundary of sustainability. The moral teaching of the seasons is that virtue must be bounded by practical wisdom—knowing when to gather in and when to share, so that life can continue rather than collapse. Option A carries weight because the family is the root unit that must survive the cycle, yet it is incomplete because it does not honor the good impulse behind the aid. Option B carries little weight here, since a heart so open that it endangers its own household ignores the harsher truth that a frozen field feeds no one. Option C best captures the seasonal insight that generosity without limits becomes destruction.

For Question 1, I allocate 25 points to A, 5 points to B, and 70 points to C. For Question 2, I allocate 20 points to A, 5 points to B, and 75 points to C.

