In a society where the seasonal calendar is the backbone of communal life, local food customs are not trivial preferences; they are the rituals that bind neighbors to the shared rhythm of nature and maintain social harmony. A restaurant that flouts these customs disrupts the moral order, because what is served and when it is served ought to follow the season, not individual commercial whim. Market autonomy therefore carries virtually no moral weight here, and while hearing grievances can be a gesture of communal care, it rings hollow if it does not carry an obligation to conform to the neighborhood’s established way of life. Preserving that character is paramount, because it is the living expression of the seasonal covenant.

